How the Depends line was derived, and why it is not ldd output
==============================================================

`ldd` reports what the linker records. It does NOT report what a process
`dlopen`s at runtime, and the two differ here in ways that matter: the GStreamer
plugin registry, the Wayland client, and xkbcommon all arrive that way. A
Depends built from ldd would install and then fail at first use.

So the list was taken from a RUNNING host: launch it, read /proc/PID/maps, map
each mapped .so back to its package with `dpkg -S`. That is what the process
actually had open, not what the linker predicted.

Derived on 2026-09-01 from a live host on Ubuntu 26.04:

    libatomic1  libc6  libffi8  libgcc-s1  libglib2.0-0t64
    libgstreamer1.0-0  libgstreamer-plugins-base1.0-0  liborc-0.4-0t64
    libpcre2-8-0  libwayland-client0  libxkbcommon0

DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED -- the GPU stack:

    libdrm2  libvulkan1

Both were mapped, and neither belongs in Depends. They arrive with whatever
driver the machine has (mesa, nvidia), and pinning them makes this package
argue with the user's driver choice for no benefit.

ADDED FROM THE DESIGN, not from the maps:

    gstreamer1.0-pipewire       provides pipewiresrc
    gstreamer1.0-plugins-base   provides videoconvert and appsink
    xdg-desktop-portal          the ScreenCast portal
    pipewire                    the stream the portal hands back

A maps snapshot only shows what has been loaded SO FAR, and capture does not
start until the operator presses Start -- so none of these appear in a snapshot
taken at idle. The pipeline is
`pipewiresrc fd={fd} path={node} ! videoconvert ! video/x-raw,format=RGBA ! ...`
in modules/module11/src/capture_linux.rs, and each element was resolved to its
package with `gst-inspect-1.0` plus `dpkg -S`.

Stated plainly because it is the one part of this list that was not measured:
it is read off the code, and it is the part most likely to rot if the pipeline
changes.

RECOMMENDS, not Depends: a portal BACKEND. xdg-desktop-portal on its own cannot
show a picker; it needs the desktop's implementation. Which one is right depends
on the desktop, so requiring any single one would be wrong.
